  "question": {
    "text": "The roots of the given equation $(p-q) x^2+(q-r) x+(r-p)=0$ are :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "G0lJr",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{p}-\\mathrm{q}}{\\mathrm{r}-\\mathrm{p}}, 1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "X5w2u",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{q}-\\mathrm{r}}{\\mathrm{p}-\\mathrm{q}}, 1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "VVwBM",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{r}-\\mathrm{p}}{\\mathrm{p}-\\mathrm{q}}, 1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Twe7j",
      "text": "None of these",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given equation is<br />$(p-q) x^2+(q-r) x+(r-p)=0$<br />By using formula for finding the root viz: $\\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4 a c}}{2 a}$, we get<br />$x=\\frac{(r-q) \\pm \\sqrt{(q-r)^2-4(r-p)(p-q)}}{2(p-q)}$<br />$\\Rightarrow \\quad x=\\frac{(r-q) \\pm(q+r-2 p)}{2(p-q)}=\\frac{r-p}{p-q}, 1$",
    "image": null
  },
